    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Before anything else, check whether you genuinely have to. A surprising share of patios people plan to strip need a clean and a recoat instead, and that distinction is worth twenty minutes of diagnosis.
        </p>

        <div id="why-strip" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Do You Actually Need to Strip?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Four situations genuinely require it, and everything else is better served by a clean and a recoat.
          </p>

          <p>
            The film has failed. Peeling, flaking, or lifting in sheets means adhesion has gone, and nothing you put on top will adhere any better than what is already there. Recoating over a failing film produces a failing film with an extra layer on it.
          </p>

          <p>
            The film has blushed and will not clear. A cloudy, milky white finish from trapped moisture sometimes resolves over warm dry days, and a solvent-based film can often be rescued with xylene to re-open it. If neither works, stripping is the remaining option. Our guide on why paver sealer turns white covers the diagnosis first, because the rescue is much cheaper than the strip.
          </p>

          <p>
            Build-up from repeated recoats. After three or four cycles a water-based film in particular can reach a thickness where the patio looks like stone under plastic, the joints are filling at the edges, and the surface feels slick. That is a strip rather than another coat.
          </p>

          <p>
            You are changing sealer type. Water-based over an existing solvent-based film does not bond and will peel, and solvent over water-based can lift it unevenly. Changing carrier means starting from bare pavers, which our comparison of the two types covers.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">CHECK FIRST</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">A worn film is not a failed film.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">If the sealer has simply thinned with age and the surface is sound, clean it and apply one thin compatible coat. That is a Saturday. Stripping is two weekends, a hundred-plus dollars of chemical, and a patio that looks worse before it looks better. Reserve it for the four situations above.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="identify" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Identify What You Are Removing
          </h2>

          <p>
            Strippers are formulated against particular chemistries, and buying the wrong one wastes a weekend.
          </p>

          <p>
            The xylene test identifies solvent-based acrylic quickly. Dab a small amount on an inconspicuous sealed area and wait a minute. If the film softens, becomes tacky or dissolves, it is solvent-based acrylic. If nothing happens, it is water-based acrylic, a polyurethane, an epoxy or a penetrating sealer.
          </p>

          <p>
            A water test tells you whether there is a film at all. Sprinkle water on the surface: strong beading indicates an intact sealer, soaking straight in suggests it is spent or that what you have is a penetrating sealer that has depleted. A penetrating sealer generally cannot and need not be stripped; it wears away and you simply reapply.
          </p>

          <p>
            Look at how it is failing, too. Acrylic films peel and flake in sheets. Polyurethane and epoxy are harder and tend to craze or chip rather than peel. Those harder coatings need considerably more aggressive strippers and are substantially more work, so identifying them before buying matters.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Test Result</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Likely Sealer</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Stripping Difficulty</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Softens with xylene</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Solvent-based acrylic</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Moderate</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Unaffected by xylene, beads water</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Water-based acrylic</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Moderate</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Very hard, crazes rather than peels</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Polyurethane or epoxy</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Difficult</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">No beading, no visible film</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Penetrating, or spent</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Usually no strip needed</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Unknown, patchy</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Mixed history</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Test in several places</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            If you genuinely cannot tell, buy a small quantity of a general-purpose paver sealer stripper and test a square foot. That hour tells you the dwell time you will need, how many applications it will take, and whether the product is working at all — which between them determine whether this is a weekend or a fortnight.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="stripper-types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Which Stripper to Buy
          </h2>

          <p>
            Three broad categories, and the right one depends on what you identified above and on your site.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Solvent-Based Strippers</h3>

          <p>
            Fast and effective on acrylic films, working in fifteen to thirty minutes. Strong odour, significant fume hazard, flammable, and hard on plants and adjacent surfaces. They are the efficient choice on an open patio with nothing growing next to it and proper respiratory protection.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Caustic or Alkaline Strippers</h3>

          <p>
            Work by breaking down the binder, similar to the caustic strippers used on decks. Effective on acrylics and some tougher coatings, lower odour than solvent, but they burn skin, damage aluminium and can etch or discolour some stone. On natural stone pavers in particular, test before committing.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Soy and Citrus-Based Strippers</h3>

          <p>
            Much slower, working over hours rather than minutes, and far gentler on plants, skin and adjacent surfaces. They genuinely work on acrylic films and they are the sensible choice for a patio surrounded by planting or in an enclosed courtyard. Budget considerably more time and expect to cover the treated area with plastic to hold the long dwell.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whatever the type, buy a thickened or gel formulation if you can. Paver sealer strippers face the same problem as deck strippers: the product must stay wet for the full dwell, and a thin liquid on a horizontal surface in sun dries long before it has worked. Gel clings and can be covered with sheeting to extend the dwell substantially.
          </p>

          <p>
            Coverage is typically 50 to 150 square feet per gallon depending on the product and how heavy the film is. Buy generously, because running out mid-section means that patch dries with partly dissolved sealer set back into it, which is harder to remove than it was originally.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="process" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. The Process
          </h2>

          <p>
            Work in sections sized to what you can scrub and rinse before the stripper dries, typically 50 to 100 square feet.
          </p>

          <p>
            Protect everything first. Cover plants with sheeting rather than just wetting them, mask adjacent render, siding, fences and any metal, and plan where the runoff will go before the first application rather than during it. Wet down and cover any concrete or stone that is not being treated, because stripper will stain and etch it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Apply generously and evenly with a brush, roller or chemical-resistant sprayer. This is not a job for a thin coat: the stripper needs enough body to stay wet and enough volume to work through the film. Cover the section with plastic sheeting if the product allows it, which dramatically extends the working dwell and is the single most effective technique available.
          </p>

          <p>
            Let it dwell for the stated time, watching rather than timing. You want the sealer to wrinkle, lift, go gummy or visibly change. Mist with water if it starts to skin over in sun. Do not let it dry, and do not massively over-dwell either, particularly with caustic products on stone.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then agitate. A stiff-bristled deck brush on a pole works for most patios; a rotary surface cleaner or a floor scrubber helps enormously on a large area. Work the softened sealer loose, then rinse it away with a pressure washer at moderate pressure, a wide 25 or 40 degree tip, held well back. High pressure blasts out joint sand and etches paver faces, and you will be re-sanding the joints anyway.
          </p>

          <p>
            Expect a second application. Most patios need two rounds, and the second should be spot work on whatever remains rather than a full reapplication. Areas that were shaded or under furniture will hold their film better than the sun-exposed centre.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">What to Budget</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">2</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Applications, typically</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">50-150</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Sq ft per gallon</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">2 weekends</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Strip, dry, reseal</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">Re-sand</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Joints, afterwards</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="rinsing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Rinsing, Neutralising and Managing the Runoff
          </h2>

          <p>
            The step people compress, and the one that determines whether the new sealer bonds.
          </p>

          <p>
            Rinse far more thoroughly than feels necessary. Stripper residue left in the paver surface will interfere with the new sealer's adhesion, and it is invisible. Rinse each section immediately after agitating, then rinse the whole patio again at the end.
          </p>

          <p>
            Neutralise if the product says to. Caustic strippers in particular leave an alkaline surface, and many manufacturers specify a neutralising rinse, often a mild acid wash, before resealing. Follow the specific product's instruction rather than a general rule, because it varies.
          </p>

          <p>
            Manage the runoff deliberately. Stripper slurry carries dissolved sealer and chemical, and it should not go into a storm drain, a watercourse, or across a lawn you care about. Check local guidance. Practically, the usual approach is to contain and collect the heavy first rinse and let the much-diluted later rinses disperse, but what is permitted varies by jurisdiction.
          </p>

          <p>
            Expect the pavers to look poor at this stage. Stripped pavers are often blotchy, discoloured and dull, with efflorescence appearing as they dry. This is normal and alarming, and most of it resolves as the surface dries fully and is cleaned. Do not judge the result until the patio is dry.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then re-sand the joints. Stripping and the rinsing that follows will have removed joint sand, and the joints need refilling and, if you are using polymeric sand, curing before any sealer goes on. Our guide on how long after polymeric sand you can seal covers the interval.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-goes-wrong" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. What Goes Wrong
          </h2>

          <p>
            Four failures, all avoidable, all common.
          </p>

          <p>
            The stripper dries before it works. The single most common error, and it leaves partly dissolved sealer re-set into the surface in a state harder to remove than the original film. Work small sections, use a gel, cover with sheeting, mist if it skins. This is the whole technique.
          </p>

          <p>
            Incomplete removal, discovered after resealing. Patches of remaining sealer produce uneven appearance and adhesion failures in the new coat. Before resealing, wet the whole patio: any area that beads still has sealer on it and needs another pass. That five-minute check prevents the worst outcome in this job.
          </p>

          <p>
            Damage from pressure. Using high pressure to speed up removal blasts out joint sand, etches paver faces and exposes aggregate, and those changes are permanent. If the sealer is not coming off, apply more stripper rather than more pressure.
          </p>

          <p>
            Resealing before the pavers are dry. After stripping, rinsing and pressure washing, pavers hold a great deal of water, and sealing over it traps moisture and produces exactly the blushed white finish you may have been stripping in the first place. Allow several dry days, longer than after an ordinary clean, and test with a taped plastic square before committing.
          </p>

          <p>
            A fifth, less common but worth knowing: caustic strippers can discolour or etch natural stone. If your pavers are travertine, limestone or another natural stone rather than concrete, test in a hidden area and consider a gentler product, because that damage does not come back.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="after" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Before You Reseal
          </h2>

          <p>
            The temptation at this point is enormous: the patio looks poor, the weekend is nearly gone, and resealing would finish it. Resist it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Wait for genuine dryness. Several dry days at minimum, more in cool or humid weather, and more again for a shaded patio. The plastic-square test is the reliable check: tape a foot square of clear plastic to the surface, sealed on all four edges, and leave it a few hours. Condensation underneath means moisture is still coming out.
          </p>

          <p>
            Clean once more. Stripping leaves residue and the rinsing lifts dirt from the joints across the surface. A final clean with an appropriate paver cleaner, followed by another dry-down, gives the new sealer a genuinely sound surface to bond to.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do the beading check across the whole patio, including under where furniture sat and in the corners. Any residual sealer will show as beading and needs another pass before you commit.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then decide deliberately what goes on next, because you have just paid a substantial price for the freedom to choose. If build-up drove the strip, consider a penetrating sealer, which never builds and never needs stripping again. If appearance drove it, our comparison of water-based and solvent-based covers which gives the look you want.
          </p>

          <p>
            And write down what you apply, with the date, somewhere durable. The commonest reason people end up stripping is nobody knowing what is on the patio, and you are now in a position to spare yourself or the next owner this entire weekend.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Stripping a Film That Only Needed Recoating</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A sealer that has simply worn thin over a sound surface wants a clean and one thin compatible coat, which is a Saturday. Stripping is two weekends and a hundred-plus dollars of chemical. Reserve it for a film that is peeling, blushed beyond rescue, badly built up, or a genuine change of sealer type.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Letting the Stripper Dry Before Agitating</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Partly dissolved sealer that re-sets into the surface is harder to remove than the original film, so a dried section becomes the worst part of the patio. Work 50 to 100 square feet at a time, use a gel product, cover the treated area with plastic sheeting, and mist if it starts to skin over.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Raising the Pressure Washer to Speed It Up</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                High pressure blasts out joint sand, etches paver faces and exposes aggregate, and all three are permanent. Use a 25 or 40 degree tip at moderate pressure held well back. If the sealer is not releasing, the answer is another application of stripper, never more force.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Resealing Before the Pavers Are Genuinely Dry</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                After stripping, rinsing and washing, pavers hold far more water than they look. Sealing over it traps moisture and produces the blushed white finish you may have been stripping in the first place. Allow several dry days and confirm with a taped plastic square before committing.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the Beading Check</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Residual patches of old sealer cause uneven appearance and adhesion failure in the new coat, and they are invisible on a dry patio. Wet the whole surface before resealing, including under where furniture sat: anything that beads still has sealer on it and needs another pass.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do you remove sealer from pavers?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Identify the sealer type, protect plants and adjacent surfaces, then apply a suitable stripper in 50 to 100 square foot sections, ideally a gel covered with plastic sheeting to hold the dwell. Agitate with a stiff brush, rinse at moderate pressure, and expect a second application on what remains.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What removes paver sealer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A dedicated paver sealer stripper, in solvent, caustic or soy and citrus form. Solvent types are fastest on acrylic films but carry fume hazards. Caustic types are effective and can etch natural stone. Soy and citrus types are far gentler on plants and skin but work over hours rather than minutes.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I have to strip old sealer before resealing?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only if the film is peeling, blushed beyond rescue, built up from repeated recoats, or you are changing from water-based to solvent-based or the reverse. A film that has simply worn thin over a sound surface should be cleaned and recoated with a compatible product instead.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you pressure wash paver sealer off?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not on its own, and trying will damage the pavers before it removes the film. Pressure washing is the rinsing step after a chemical stripper has softened the sealer. High pressure blasts out joint sand, etches faces and exposes aggregate permanently, so use a wide tip at moderate pressure held well back.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long after stripping can I reseal pavers?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Several dry days at minimum, longer in cool or humid weather and longer again for a shaded patio, because stripping and rinsing leave pavers holding a great deal of water. Confirm with a taped square of clear plastic: condensation underneath after a few hours means moisture is still coming out.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I know what sealer is on my pavers?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Dab xylene on an inconspicuous sealed area: if the film softens or goes tacky it is solvent-based acrylic, and if nothing happens it is likely water-based, a polyurethane or a penetrating sealer. A water test also helps — strong beading means an intact film, while soaking straight in suggests a spent or penetrating sealer.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does stripping damage the pavers?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Done properly, no. Done with excessive pressure it permanently etches faces and exposes aggregate, and caustic strippers can discolour or etch natural stone such as travertine and limestone. Test any caustic product in a hidden area first if your pavers are stone rather than concrete.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

