        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Check first whether you genuinely need to strip, because a film that has merely worn thin wants a clean and one compatible coat instead. Stripping is for a film that is peeling, blushed beyond rescue, built up from repeated recoats, or when you are changing between water-based and solvent-based, which do not bond to each other.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            If you are committed, the whole technique is keeping the stripper wet: small sections, a gel product, plastic sheeting over the treated area, and mist it if it skins. Then rinse at moderate pressure rather than blasting, wet the patio to find any residual beading, and wait several genuinely dry days before resealing. Sealing damp pavers after all that work produces the blushed white finish you may have been stripping in the first place.
          </p>
